1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:05,431
You would never make a cook reread a 500-page recipe binder before frying one egg.

2
00:00:05,731 --> 00:00:07,734
But that is how you prompt your AI agent.

3
00:00:08,034 --> 00:00:09,291
Agent Skills fix that.

4
00:00:09,791 --> 00:00:14,679
In your kitchen, every rule you wrote sits in one giant binder: the one big prompt.

5
00:00:14,979 --> 00:00:17,205
Your cook reads all of it before every order.

6
00:00:17,705 --> 00:00:19,314
The fix is a recipe box.

7
00:00:19,614 --> 00:00:23,464
Every recipe is a card with a tab: its name, and when to use it.

8
00:00:23,764 --> 00:00:25,328
Your cook reads only the tabs.

9
00:00:25,628 --> 00:00:26,556
Soup is ordered.

10
00:00:26,856 --> 00:00:28,914
He pulls the soup card, and nothing else.

11
00:00:29,414 --> 00:00:31,100
Agent Skills work the same way.

12
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:34,820
A skill is a folder with one file: SKILL.md.

13
00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:38,986
At startup, your agent loads only each skill's name and description.

14
00:00:39,286 --> 00:00:42,080
When a task matches, it reads the full instructions.

15
00:00:42,380 --> 00:00:44,390
Extra files load only if needed.

16
00:00:44,890 --> 00:00:48,172
So it keeps many skills on hand, and a clear head for the job.

17
00:00:48,672 --> 00:00:51,468
It is an open standard, started by Anthropic.

18
00:00:51,768 --> 00:00:53,893
But the lesson is as old as the kitchen.

19
00:00:54,193 --> 00:00:55,729
Don't tell your agent everything.

20
00:00:56,029 --> 00:00:56,917
Tell it where to look.

21
00:00:57,417 --> 00:00:58,819
Subscribe for the next one.
